<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 550–330 BCE (from Cyrus the Great to Darius III)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From King of Kings to Slave</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/apadana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apadana.jpg" alt="Apadana"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/apadana">Apadana</a></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, the largest empire of its time, ranked people by their closeness to the king and by whether they belonged to the ruling people. At the top stood the King of Kings, who ruled “by the grace of Ahuramazda” and called even his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> his bandaka, men bound to him by loyalty. Around him were the royal kin and the great Persian houses, who supplied most satraps and generals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> of the homeland, a warrior people, paid no tribute, while the many subject peoples, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, kept their own laws, gods and often their own rulers, but owed the king tribute, labour and soldiers. Below them came dependent workers, the kurtaš, who lived on royal rations, and slaves, who were few beside the free farmers and craftsmen. Royal favour could raise a man quickly; disloyalty could destroy a whole family.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 550–330 BCE (from Cyrus the Great to Darius III)</span><span><b>People</b> 17–35 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 8</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The King of Kings</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Royal kin and the great houses</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Satraps and high officials</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Magi</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Persian warriors</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:100%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Tribute-paying subjects</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-dependent soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Workers on rations (kurtaš)</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Slaves</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
