<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/darius-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The King of Kings <span class="soc-term">xšāyaθiya xšāyaθiyānām (King of Kings)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The King of Kings ruled a huge empire, and people from many lands brought him gifts.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a> was, in the words of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>’s tomb, “King of Kings, King of countries containing all kinds of men”, and he ruled, as Darius proclaimed at Behistun, “by the grace of Ahuramazda”. He commanded the armies, appointed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> and generals, and was the source of the king’s law (dāta). His court moved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and the reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apadana">Apadana</a> at Persepolis show delegations of the subject peoples bringing him gifts. The king rewarded loyalty with land, gold and office and punished disloyalty with death, at times of a whole family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> founded the empire by defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> in 550 BCE; Darius I, who seized the throne in 522 BCE with the help of six Persian nobles, organised it into satrapies with fixed tribute. Four kings lie in rock-cut tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>. Kingship could be dangerous: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> was murdered in his bed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a>, Greek writers say, was poisoned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagoas">Bagoas</a> in 338 BCE.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The throne stayed in the Achaemenid family and usually went to a son of the king, but rivals fought for it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> claimed descent from a side branch of the family, and Artaxerxes II had to defeat his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a> at Cunaxa in 401 BCE.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One king, with the royal family.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apadana">Apadana</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cyrus-the-younger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Royal kin and the great houses <span class="soc-term">Haxāmanišiya (Achaemenid)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Noble Persian families helped the king rule, leading his armies and governing his provinces.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the king stood his own clan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, and a small circle of great Persian houses. Six of them descended from the nobles who joined Darius in 522 BCE to kill the man he called the usurper Gaumata: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otanes">Otanes</a>, Intaphernes, Gobryas, Hydarnes, Megabyzus and Aspathines. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, these men could enter the king’s presence unannounced unless he was with a woman, and Darius bound their houses to his by marriage: he married daughters of Otanes and Gobryas, and Gobryas’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> married a daughter of Darius. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> under Darius came from the royal house or the six families, and nobles commanded the armies. Their wealth came from estates across the empire, from the king’s gifts and from office: Darius’s uncle Parnaka ran the economy of the homeland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> in 506–497 BCE, and his son Artabazus founded a line of satraps in Hellespontine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>. In Herodotus’s account, when Intaphernes attacked two palace officers, Darius had him and almost all his family put to death.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth and marriage decided who belonged, and the king tied the great houses to the throne by marrying their daughters and giving them his own; a noble who lost his trust could lose his life and his family.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: the king’s kin and a few great families with their households, in an empire of 17–35 million people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otanes">Otanes</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabazus-i-of-phrygia">Artabazus I of Phrygia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/pharnabazus-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pharnabazus-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Satraps and high officials <span class="soc-term">xšaçapāvan (satrap), ganzabara (treasurer)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Satraps governed the empire’s provinces for the king, collecting taxes and keeping the roads safe.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> (xšaçapāvan, “protector of the province”) governed each of the empire’s 20 to 30 provinces with an almost royal court of his own. He collected the tribute, kept the roads safe, judged cases and kept troops; but his secretary, his treasurer and the commander of the garrisons reported directly to the king, and royal inspectors toured the provinces. In the Behistun inscription Darius calls his satraps his bandaka, men bound to him by loyalty, and they held office only by his favour: according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oroetes">Oroetes</a>, satrap at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, put to death for acting on his own, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryandes">Aryandes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for minting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins that rivalled his own. Later some satrapies passed from father to son, and in the 4th century satraps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> rose in open revolt. Many non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> served the king too: the Egyptian priest and physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udjahorresnet">Udjahorresnet</a> advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> and Darius, and the Jewish courtier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a>, by his own account, was sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The king appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>, mostly from the Persian nobility, and could recall or execute them; lower offices were open to men of many peoples who could serve him, from the Elamite and Babylonian scribes of the treasuries to Egyptian and Jewish courtiers.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: there were between 20 and 30 satrapies at different times, but the officials under the satraps and at court were never counted.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oroetes">Oroetes</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryandes">Aryandes</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnabazus-ii">Pharnabazus II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udjahorresnet">Udjahorresnet</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrapy</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-satraps-revolt">Great Satraps' Revolt</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zoroastrianism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Magi <span class="soc-term">magu- (magus), Greek magoi</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Magi were Persian priests who led prayers and offerings to the gods.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The magi were the priests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> calls them one of the tribes of the Medes, says that no Persian could make an offering without a magus present to sing a hymn of the gods’ birth, and describes them interpreting dreams and omens for the kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> tablets show magi and other priests drawing grain, wine and animals from the royal stores for offerings to Ahuramazda and to Elamite gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humban">Humban</a> alike, for the administration provided for all of them. Darius’s inscriptions name Ahuramazda as the god who gave him his kingdom, and it was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> reached south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and became a defining part of Persian culture, though how closely the kings and the magi followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a>’s teaching is debated. The magi could also fall under suspicion: Darius claimed that the usurper he overthrew in 522 BCE, Gaumata, was a magus, and Herodotus tells of a massacre of magi after his death, remembered in a festival called the Magophonia.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Magi were born into their priestly tribe; the most trusted served at court, where, Greek writers say, they interpreted the king’s dreams and helped to educate royal princes.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: Herodotus calls the magi one of the tribes of the Medes, but their numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Persian warriors <span class="soc-term">kāra (people, army), Greek athanatoi (Immortals)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Persian men formed the heart of the king’s army, and the Greeks called his best troops the Immortals.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> (Pārsa), in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, were the ruling people of the empire, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> one word, kāra, meant both “people” and “army”. Their homeland paid no tribute, and its men formed the core of the king’s forces: according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Persian boys were taught from the age of five to twenty to ride, to shoot the bow and to speak the truth. The best served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a>, a corps of 10,000 heavy infantry, mostly Persians with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, which Herodotus says was kept at full strength by replacing at once every man who died or fell sick; a thousand of them guarded the king. Persian troops manned garrisons across the empire, and Persian nobles commanded the contingents of the subject peoples in war. Between campaigns most Persians lived as farmers and herders.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Persian birth made a man one of the ruling people; service in the king’s wars could bring gifts and promotion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> himself, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, had served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> as a spear-bearer before he seized the throne.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: Herodotus puts the Immortals at 10,000, but the Persians of the homeland were never counted.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cyrus-cylinder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>Most people (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Tribute-paying subjects <span class="soc-term">dahyāva (the lands), bāji (tribute)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people in the empire were farmers and craftspeople from many lands, who paid taxes to the king.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most of the empire’s people, farmers, herders, craftsmen and townspeople, lived in its subject lands, the dahyāva, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>; the Behistun inscription lists twenty-three of them. They kept their own languages, laws and temples and often their own rulers, such as the Phoenician kings and the Greek tyrants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> served as the common written language of government. To the king they owed tribute, which Darius fixed for each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>: according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Babylonia paid 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> a year and fed the king’s army for four months, and Egypt paid 700 talents and 120,000 measures of grain. Subjects also sent levies to the army and labour to the royal works. Many held land in return for service: in 5th-century Babylonia, holders of “bow land”, “horse land” and “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> land” owed soldiers to the king, and many dealt with the Murashu firm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, which leased their plots and paid their taxes.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Service to the king, as soldier, scribe or courtier, could lift a subject; rebellion brought the opposite, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes the people of rebel cities, such as Barca and Miletus, enslaved or deported.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other ranks: the farmers, herders, craftsmen and townspeople of the subject lands and their families, the great majority of 17–35 million people. Because no rank below the king has a reliable count, this also takes in the nobles, officials, magi, Persians, kurtaš and slaves.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murashu-archive">Murashu archive</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-dependent" id="rank-7"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis-fortification-tablets" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/persepolis-fortification-tablets.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Serfs and dependants</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Workers on rations (kurtaš) <span class="soc-term">kurtaš (Elamite)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Workers from many lands built the palaces of Persepolis and were paid in food such as grain and wine.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> Fortification tablets, written in Elamite between 509 and 493 BCE, record the rations that the royal administration of the homeland issued each month to its workers, the kurtaš. They came from across the empire: Darius’s own account of his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> names Ionian and Lydian stonecutters, Median and Egyptian goldsmiths and Babylonian brickmakers. Women appear in the tablets as often as men. Rations were paid in grain, with wine, beer or meat for some, and from 492 BCE the Treasury tablets record <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> paid in place of part of the rations. Women generally received less than men, but skilled craftswomen were paid as much as men, some women who led work groups received more than any man in them, and new mothers drew extra rations, twice as much for a boy as for a girl. Great households had workforces of their own: the wealthy Irdabama had 480 labourers near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. How free the kurtaš were is debated, for the word could mean hired or bound workers.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Some kurtaš were deportees brought in to work on the royal building projects; skill and the leadership of a work group brought higher rations.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: the Persepolis tablets of 509–493 BCE record work groups in the Persian homeland, such as Irdabama’s 480 labourers near Shiraz, but no total exists.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis-fortification-tablets">Persepolis Fortification Tablets</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">8</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Slaves <span class="soc-term">qallu (Babylonian), māniya- (Old Persian, household servant)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Slaves were owned by other people and had to work without pay; many were captives taken in war.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was an old institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Media, and it continued under Persian rule. People became slaves through war and rebellion, by birth to a slave mother, by sale, and in Babylonia through debt: a creditor could take a defaulting debtor’s children as slaves, though debtors no longer pledged or sold themselves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes the enslavement of rebellious cities, and says that Babylonia sent the king 500 boys a year as part of its tribute. Persian nobles with estates in Babylonia and other conquered lands owned many slaves, and contracts of sale survive from Babylonia, where sales were registered and taxed; one tablet in Babylonian records a slave bought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> itself under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. Slaves worked mainly in households and workshops, and some Babylonian slaves ran businesses of their own and paid their owners a fixed income. In the historian Muhammad Dandamayev’s judgement, slaves were few beside the free farmers, tenants and craftsmen on whose labour the empire rested.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Owners could free slaves by a written deed, often on condition that the freed person support them for the rest of their lives; children born to slave women were slaves.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: the historian Muhammad Dandamayev judged that slaves were few compared with free people, but gave no figure.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="achaemenid-persia">Be born in the Persian Empire of Darius I</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Birth ranked people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, but the king’s favour could change a life quickly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> once a spear-bearer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>, seized the throne in 522 BCE with six fellow nobles, and their families remained among the greatest in the empire. The king rewarded service with land, gold and office, and he gave them to foreigners too: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> served Darius as soldiers, craftsmen and advisers, the exiled Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> received the revenues of three cities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a>, and non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udjahorresnet">Udjahorresnet</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> rose high in royal service. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagoas">Bagoas</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a>, became so powerful that he is said to have poisoned two kings and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> on the throne. Falls were as sudden. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a> who acted on their own were executed, rebel cities were enslaved or deported, as the people of Barca were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> debt could cost a family its children.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A woman’s place depended on her family’s rank. Royal women were ranked in a strict order, from the king’s mother and the queen to his daughters and concubines, and kings married women of the royal family or of the great Persian houses; concubines were often slaves, captives or foreign princesses whose sons could not inherit. Greek writers imagined the royal women shut away in a harem, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> tablets show royal and noble women owning estates and workshops, travelling with their own staff and managing large workforces, as Irdabama did with 480 labourers near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. Queens such as Atossa and Parysatis had real influence, though no woman ever ruled. Among the workers of the homeland women were as numerous as men; they were generally paid less, but skilled craftswomen earned as much as men, and women who led work groups more. Elsewhere women lived under the customs of their own peoples, which differed from land to land.</p></section>
