agi were Persian priests who led prayers and offerings to the gods.","rise":"Magi were born into their priestly tribe; the most trusted served at court, where, Greek writers say, they interpreted the king’s dreams and helped to educate royal princes.","why":"The magi were the priests of the Persians and Medes.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":5,"band":"specialists","name":"Persian warriors","term":"kāra (people, army), Greek athanatoi (Immortals)","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":0,"pic":"","you":"You are a Persian of the homeland, taught as a boy to ride, shoot the bow and speak the truth; your land pays no tribute, but when the king calls up the army you march, and if you are among the best you serve in the Immortals.","kid":"Persian men formed the heart of the king’s army, and the Greeks called his best troops the Immortals.","rise":"Persian birth made a man one of the ruling people; service in the king’s wars could bring gifts and promotion, and Darius I himself, according to Herodotus, had served Cambyses II as a spear-bearer before he seized the throne.","why":"The Persians of Persis (Pārsa), in south-western Iran, were the ruling people of the empire, and in Old Persian one word, kāra, meant both “people” and “army”.","people":[]},{"n":6,"lv":6,"band":"commoners","name":"Tribute-paying subjects","term":"dahyāva (the lands), bāji (tribute)","lbl":"Most people (the rest)","w":100,"firm":0,"most":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cyrus-cylinder.jpg","you":"You farm or work at a trade in one of the subject lands, under your own laws and gods; each year your satrapy sends the king its tribute, and you may be called to serve in his army or on his building works.","kid":"Most people in the empire were farmers and craftspeople from many lands, who paid taxes to the king.","rise":"Service to the king, as soldier, scribe or courtier, could lift a subject; rebellion brought the opposite, and Herodotus describes the people of rebel cities, such as Barca and Miletus, enslaved or deported.","why":"Most of the empire’s people, farmers, herders, craftsmen and townspeople, lived in its subject lands, the dahyāva, from Egypt and Ionia to Babylonia, Bactria and the Indus; the Behistun inscription lists twenty-three…","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":7,"band":"dependent","name":"Workers on rations (kurtaš)","term":"kurtaš (Elamite)","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":0,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/persepolis-fortification-tablets.jpg","you":"You work on the king’s buildings or estates near Persepolis and draw a monthly ration of grain and wine from his storehouses; if you are a skilled craftswoman, you are paid as much as the men.","kid":"Workers from many lands built the palaces of Persepolis and were paid in food such as grain and wine.","rise":"Some kurtaš were deportees brought in to work on the royal building projects; skill and the leadership of a work group brought higher rations.","why":"The Persepolis Fortification tablets, written in Elamite between 509 and 493 BCE, record the rations that the royal administration of the homeland issued each month to its workers, the kurtaš.","people":[]},{"n":8,"lv":8,"band":"enslaved","name":"Slaves","term":"qallu (Babylonian), māniya- (Old Persian, household servant)","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You are owned by a Babylonian household or a Persian noble’s estate, which can sell you by a registered and taxed contract; if your owner allows, you may run a business of your own and pay a fixed sum to your master.","kid":"Slaves were owned by other people and had to work without pay; many were captives taken in war.","rise":"Owners could free slaves by a written deed, often on condition that the freed person support them for the rest of their lives; children born to slave women were slaves.","why":"Slavery was an old institution in Babylonia, Egypt and Media, and it continued under Persian rule.","people":[]}]}</script>
